{
 "refId": "valve-testing",
 "version": "1.0.0",
 "source": "API Standard 598, Valve Inspection and Testing, Tenth Edition, 2023. Tables 1 through 5. Transcribed from a purchased copy held outside version control.",
 "note": "The first reference pack that produces ITP rows rather than drawing figures. B16.34 rates the valve and B16.10 measures it; API 598 says what has to be witnessed before anyone accepts it.",
 "units": {
  "pressure": "barG",
  "alternatePressure": "psig",
  "duration": "seconds"
 },
 "conventions": {
  "shellPressureIsComputedNotTabulated": "For steel and nonferrous valves Table 2 gives no number. It gives a rule: 1.5 x the pressure rating at 38 C, rounded up to the next whole bar. The rating comes from ASME B16.34, so this pack and valve-pt-ratings have to be read together or the answer cannot be produced at all.",
  "twoAxesTheRepoDidNotHave": "API 598 tests a FLOATING ball valve differently from a TRUNNION MOUNTED one, and an API 609 Category A butterfly differently from a Category B. Neither distinction exists on a PMS row today. The pack records the variant on every requirement so a consumer can refuse to answer for a bare \"BALL\" rather than quietly picking one.",
  "highAndLowInvert": "Read Table 1 as a pair. Wherever the low-pressure closure test becomes optional - above Class 1500 for small valves, above Class 600 for large ones - the high-pressure closure test becomes required. The two rows are the same decision written twice.",
  "classThresholdMovesWithSize": "The class band that splits the closure rows is NOT the same for both sizes: 1500 for DN <= 100 and 600 for DN > 100. Treating it as one threshold mis-states every large valve between Class 600 and 1500.",
  "leakageIsByNominalSize": "API 598 5.9.2.6: leakage rates are based on the DN (NPS) of the valve regardless of whether it is full or reduced bore."
 },
